  <CaseSection id="process" title="Engineering">
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        The wake word is detected on the device, so no audio leaves the device to
        listen for “Logatot.” Providers asked about this first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Speech is matched against known phrases and the class roster without AI. A
        live model would be slower and could log a nap that never happened. The
        rules either match an entry or send it to review.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI runs only when a child is added. It lists likely mishearings of the name,
        and corrections in the review queue add to that list. Spanish is translated
        before matching, so one phrase set covers both languages.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards label="Services" items={integrations.map((s) => ({ icon: s.icon, title: s.name, body: s.role }))} />

    <CaseProse class="mt-12">
      <p>
        Attendance is calculated from check-in history, so it always matches what
        happened. A wrong headcount is a licensing problem.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <Heading level={3} visual="h2" class="mb-6">System-level decisions</Heading>
      <ul class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {structural.map((item) => (
          <li class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-ink">
            <Icon name={item.icon} size={20} class="shrink-0 text-ink-muted" />
            {item.label}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <Fragment slot="media">
      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2" id="process-htmx" class="scroll-mt-32">htmx and Alpine instead of Hotwire</Heading>
        <p>
          Rails 7 comes with Hotwire: Turbo for updating pages and Stimulus for JavaScript. I
          chose htmx and Alpine instead. Turbo and Stimulus were switched off two days into the
          front-end work, and nothing in the app uses them.
        </p>
        <p>
          With htmx, a piece of markup asks the server for HTML and says where to put it. The
          server answers from an ordinary controller action with an ordinary partial, and one
          helper, <code>htmx_request?</code>, tells the action which to send. That made it easy
          to load parts of a page from the server and refresh them when something changed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Alpine keeps a widget’s state in the markup that renders it. The back-end developer
          found that easier than Stimulus, where the behavior lives in a separate controller
          file connected by data attributes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things made them the better fit. With htmx, the server stays the only thing that
          renders HTML, and a response can name what happened instead of knowing every part of the
          page it changes. With Alpine, a widget’s state and behavior sit in the markup that draws
          it, so a Rails developer reads and changes one file.
        </p>
        <p>
          77 views use htmx attributes and about 75 hold an Alpine component. The app’s own
          JavaScript is 16 files and about 1,350 lines. In each pair below, the left side is the
          shipped code. The right side is illustrative: Logatot never had a Hotwire version, so I
          wrote it to show what the same feature would take.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={4} visual="h3">A panel that refreshes itself</Heading>
        <p>
          The Voiceboard’s shift panel loads when the page opens. When another device logs a
          shift, Pusher fires an event and the panel asks for itself again. The controller action
          is a plain query and a partial.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Why htmx.</strong> The panel decides when it
          reloads, so nothing else in the app has to know it exists.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-8">
        <div class="code-stack">
          {panel.map((s, i) => (
            <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === panel.length - 1 ? 'Three pieces: the markup names its URL and its triggers, the action returns a partial, and a Pusher message fires the event.' : undefined} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={4} visual="h3">Saving a form in a modal</Heading>
        <p>
          Saving a shift closes the modal, shows a snackbar, and refreshes the shift panel. With
          htmx, the save answers with the names of those events, and the panel above reloads
          itself. With Turbo Streams, the save has to load the panel’s data and render it, so
          every form that changes the panel has to know about it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Why htmx.</strong> The save doesn’t need to know
          what’s on the Voiceboard. A new panel can listen for the same event without anyone
          changing a form.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-8">
        <div class="code-grid">
          <div class="code-stack">
            <p class="mono-label">htmx, as shipped</p>
            {modal.real.map((s: Snippet, i: number) => (
              <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === modal.real.length - 1 ? 'One response header closes the modal, shows the snackbar, and reloads the panel. Existing listeners do the rest.' : undefined} />
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="code-stack">
            <p class="mono-label">Hotwire, illustrative</p>
            {modal.hotwire.map((s: Snippet, i: number) => (
              <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === modal.hotwire.length - 1 ? 'The save loads and renders the panel itself. Closing the dialog would also need a small Stimulus controller, and the Turbo version the app shipped with (7.3) had no whole-page refresh to fall back on.' : undefined} />
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={4} visual="h3">Filters that update as you type</Heading>
        <p>
          Expense filters update the list as you type, and the address keeps the filters, so a
          filtered list can be shared. The form is a real GET form, so it still works without
          JavaScript.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Why htmx.</strong> Everything the filter does is
          in one form tag a Rails developer can read. With Turbo, the typing behavior lives in a
          JavaScript file that has to be found and kept in step with the form.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-8">
        <div class="code-grid">
          <div class="code-stack">
            <p class="mono-label">htmx, as shipped</p>
            {filters.real.map((s: Snippet, i: number) => (
              <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === filters.real.length - 1 ? 'The wait between keystrokes, the target, and the address all live in the form tag. The action sends just the results when htmx asks.' : undefined} />
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="code-stack">
            <p class="mono-label">Hotwire, illustrative</p>
            {filters.hotwire.map((s: Snippet, i: number) => (
              <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === filters.hotwire.length - 1 ? 'Turbo can render the full page and keep only the frame, so the action wouldn’t change. It has no wait between keystrokes, though, so typing needs a Stimulus controller.' : undefined} />
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={4} visual="h3">Selecting rows to delete</Heading>
        <p>
          Rows can be selected and deleted together, and the confirm counts the selection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Why Alpine.</strong> The back-end developer edits
          one partial and sees the whole widget. In Stimulus, the same change spans the partial, a
          controller file, and target and action names that have to match between them.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-8">
        <div class="code-grid">
          <div class="code-stack">
            <p class="mono-label">Alpine, as shipped</p>
            {bulk.real.map((s: Snippet, i: number) => (
              <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === bulk.real.length - 1 ? 'State, the select-all rule, and the confirm text sit in the partial. The delete itself is htmx.' : undefined} />
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="code-stack">
            <p class="mono-label">Stimulus, illustrative</p>
            {bulk.hotwire.map((s: Snippet, i: number) => (
              <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === bulk.hotwire.length - 1 ? 'The same logic moves to a controller file, and the markup gains a target and an action for every piece.' : undefined} />
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={4} visual="h3">Alpine and htmx together</Heading>
        <p>
          On the Voiceboard, Alpine tracks which attendance pill is open, and htmx loads that
          pill’s list of children when it opens. The list action answers only htmx requests and
          sends a direct visit back to the Voiceboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Why both.</strong> Alpine handles what only the
          browser knows, which pill is open. htmx handles what only the server knows, which
          children are absent.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-8">
        <div class="code-stack">
          {pills.map((s, i) => (
            <CodeBlock filename={s.file} lang={s.lang} code={s.code} caption={i === pills.length - 1 ? 'Alpine decides what is open; htmx fetches what goes in it.' : undefined} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </Container>


      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2">The shared confirm script</Heading>
        <p>
          The confirm dialog and the double-submit guard are one script. It reads the
          attributes the views already used, so existing markup started working without
          edits.
        </p>
        <CaseCards label="How a view asks for confirmation" items={confirmApi} />
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <div class="code-grid">
          <CodeBlock
            filename="registrations/edit.html.erb (before)"
            lang="erb"
            code={confirmBefore}
            caption="Neither attribute did anything: Turbo was commented out and rails-ujs was never loaded."
          />
          <CodeBlock
            filename="registrations/edit.html.erb (after)"
            lang="erb"
            code={confirmAfter}
            caption="The shared dialog reads data-confirm, with a translated question and a named action."
          />
        </div>
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

